      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Given a choice between receiving 1,000,000 UZS today or 1,000,000 UZS one year from now, a rational person always chooses today. Not out of impatience or greed — but because today's money can be invested to earn returns, its purchasing power is higher, and it carries no collection risk. This preference for present money over future money is called the time preference for money, and it is the foundation of everything in this unit. A Concrete Illustration Let's make this very concrete. Suppose you lend a friend 2,000,000 UZS today. He promises to return it in 3 years. What is the \"fair\" repayment? If he returns exactly 2,000,000 UZS, you have actually lost money. Here is why: • You gave up the ability to invest that money for 3 years. At a conservative 12% per year bank deposit, 2,000,000 UZS would have grown to 2,000,000 × (1.12)3 = 2,809,856 UZS. • Inflation at 8% per year means 2,000,000 UZS in 3 years buys what approximately 1,587,600 UZS buys today. • You took on the risk that your friend might not pay at all. The fair repayment is approximately 2,809,856 UZS — not 2,000,000. The extra 809,856 UZS is the compensation for time, inflation, and risk. This is exactly what interest is — not an arbitrary fee, but the mathematically correct price of borrowing money across time. — n WORKED EXAMPLE — WORKED EXAMPLE — TVM in a Real Uzbekistan Context Scenario: You have 10,000,000 UZS in cash under your mattress for 2 years. Bank deposit rate: 14% per year. Inflation: 9% per year. After 2 years, your cash is still 10,000,000 UZS nominally. BUT: If you had deposited it, you would have: 10,000,000 × (1.14)² = 12,996,000 UZS. AND: Due to inflation, your 10,000,000 UZS only buys what 8,416,800 UZS bought 2 years ago. Net result of holding cash: You \"lost\" approximately 4,579,200 UZS in opportunity terms. Lesson: There is no such thing as \"safe\" cash under a mattress. Inaction is a decision with a cost. Why This Matters for Every Financial Decision Once you understand TVM, you realize that every financial decision involves comparing money at different points in time. This is true whether you are: • Deciding whether to buy a car now or save for it • Evaluating whether a business investment is worthwhile • Choosing between two job offers with different pay structures • Deciding when to buy a house • Comparing investment options with different time horizons In every case, to compare fairly, you must convert all cash flows to the same point in time. That is what Present Value and Future Value calculations allow us to do."
