      "text": "n=3 Step-by-Step: How to Calculate PV Let's work through a complete calculation step by step, so you can apply it to any situation. — n WORKED EXAMPLE — WORKED EXAMPLE — Full PV Calculation Problem: You will receive 15,000,000 UZS in 4 years. You can earn 10% per year on alternative investments. What is 15,000,000 UZS worth to you TODAY? Step 1: Identify the inputs. FV = 15,000,000 r = 0.10 n = 4 Step 2: Calculate the discount factor. (1 + r)^n = (1.10)^4 = 1.10 × 1.10 × 1.10 × 1.10 = 1.4641 Step 3: Divide FV by the discount factor. PV = 15,000,000 ÷ 1.4641 = 10,245,181 UZS Interpretation: Receiving 15,000,000 UZS in 4 years is equivalent to receiving 10,245,181 UZS today, assuming you can earn 10% per year. If someone wants to sell you a right to receive 15M in 4 years, you should pay NO MORE than 10,245,181 UZS for it. How the Discount Rate Affects PV The discount rate is the most powerful variable in the PV formula. Even small changes in the rate produce large differences in present value, especially over long time horizons. The higher the rate, the less future money is worth today. Discount Rate PV of 10M UZS in 1 year PV of 10M UZS in 5 years PV of 10M UZS in 10 years 5% 9,523,810 UZS 7,835,262 UZS 6,139,133 UZS 10% 9,090,909 UZS 6,209,213 UZS 3,855,433 UZS 15% 8,695,652 UZS 4,971,767 UZS 2,471,847 UZS 20% 8,333,333 UZS 4,018,776 UZS 1,615,055 UZS 25% 8,000,000 UZS 3,276,800 UZS 1,073,742 UZS Notice: at 25% discount rate over 10 years, 10,000,000 UZS in the future is worth only 1,073,742 UZS today — about 11 cents on the dollar. This illustrates why high-risk investments (which require high discount rates) place much less value on distant future cash flows. What Is the Right Discount Rate to Use? Choosing the correct discount rate is one of the most important and debated questions in finance. The discount rate should represent the opportunity cost of capital — the return you could earn on an alternative investment of similar risk. • For a very safe investment: use a low rate (e.g., 10–14% in Uzbekistan, matching bank deposits) • For a medium-risk investment (real estate, established business): use 15–20% • For a high-risk investment (startup, crypto): use 25–40% or higher • For valuing corporate projects: use the company's cost of capital (covered in Unit 3)"
